NOT APPLIED. NOT TO BE SUBMITTED UPSTREAM. Kept as a record.
=============================================================================

This is a working fix for two defects in FAudio 26.07/26.08's resampler. It is
correct: verified under AddressSanitizer, mutation-tested in both directions,
and a race survived 8/8 with it where it had survived 0/8 without. It is
deliberately unused, and nothing in the build applies it.

WHY IT IS NOT USED

  extern/faudio is pinned to 26.06 instead, which predates the rewrite that
  introduced both defects and therefore does not have them at all. Carrying a
  private fork of an audio library to work around a two-month-old regression is
  a worse long-term trade than pinning to the last release without it.
  tools/setup-faudio-macos.sh carries the full account of both bugs and how
  they were measured.

WHY IT MUST NOT GO UPSTREAM

  FAudio's contribution policy forbids AI-generated code -- see CLAUDE.md in
  the FAudio repository. This patch was written by an AI assistant, so it
  cannot be offered to that project, and re-typing it by hand from this file
  would not change that.

  What the policy does permit is REPORTING the issue: it explicitly allows AI
  to identify problems, provided a human independently confirms them before
  filing. There is a strong case to report here -- an ASan trace naming the
  write, a mutation test, and src/AudioSweep as a two-second reproducer -- and
  doing so would be the useful thing. The fix itself would need a different
  author.

WHY IT IS KEPT AT ALL

  It is the only copy. It was removed from the tree when the version pin
  replaced it, and survived solely inside commit c4c20f7's diff -- one squash
  away from being lost. If a newer FAudio ever fixes only one of the two
  defects, or if someone wants to write their own, this is the analysis in
  executable form.

  It is emphatically NOT an example of what not to do. The patch was right. It
  was rejected for licensing and maintenance reasons that have nothing to do
  with its quality, and mislabelling it as a mistake would mislead whoever
  finds it next.

WHAT IT FIXES, against FAudio 26.08's src/FAudio_internal.c

  1. FAudio_INTERNAL_MixSource's resample tap loops are unbounded against the
     buffer they write into. They emit one output frame per iteration and stop
     when the source offset reaches prev_sample_count, advancing by
     resampleStep each time, so they run about (leftover samples / step) times
     -- while the destination is sized to the *output* quantum, which does not
     depend on the frequency ratio. A low enough ratio overruns it. ASan:
     heap-buffer-overflow, WRITE of size 4, zero bytes past a 1764-byte region,
     which is a 441-frame quantum of floats exactly.

  2. toDecode is computed as (offset + 1 - totalSamples) into a uint64_t and
     goes negative when the ratio *falls* between quanta -- the source is
     consumed more slowly than before, so the samples already decoded exceed
     what the new ratio needs. It wraps to ~2^64 and trips the decodeSamples
     assertion in FAudio_INTERNAL_DecodeBuffers.

  The game reaches both: every car in db.xml carries
  <rpmFreqRange>0 1</rpmFreqRange> and SoundMotor::OnMotor computes the ratio
  as x + alpha*(y - x), so the ratio *is* alpha -- a sweep from 0 at minRPM to
  1 at maxRPM, rising and falling with the engine.

=============================================================================

